*Part of my [Build in Public](https://dev.to/zaerohell) series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.*

*Repo: `zaerohell/content-automation` · 2026-10-19*

\#playadev #buildinpublic
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next.js 16.4 made Cache Components the recommended path, switched them on by default for new &lt;code&gt;create-next-app&lt;/code&gt; projects, and shipped &lt;code&gt;next upgrade --agent&lt;/code&gt; so an AI agent can drive the upgrade for you. Bumping the version number takes a minute. Moving an existing app onto the new caching model is a migration, and nobody runs it for you, not even the agent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Existing apps do not adopt the new caching model on their own. Upgrading the package and adopting Cache Components are two separate jobs, and the second one changes what your pages are allowed to cache.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cache Components flip the default question from "which routes are dynamic?" to "which components and functions do I explicitly cache?" That is a contract change, not a config change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;next upgrade --agent&lt;/code&gt; is useful for the mechanical edits. It cannot decide how fresh your data has to be, so keep that decision with a human.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What 16.4 actually changes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three things land in this release that matter for a production app:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cache Components become the recommended path.&lt;/strong&gt; New projects created with &lt;code&gt;create-next-app&lt;/code&gt; get them by default, so the docs, the examples and the next hire's muscle memory all move to this model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;next upgrade --agent&lt;/code&gt; exists.&lt;/strong&gt; The upgrade tool now has an agent oriented mode built for coding agents to run the migration steps.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Builds got faster for at least one real team.&lt;/strong&gt; An independent production upgrade write up from Roboto Studio reported roughly 5% less shipped JavaScript after moving to 16.4, and a 1,555 page benchmark went from 83.9s total build time on 16.3 to 49.1s on 16.4.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That third point is a reason to measure your own app, not a promise. Your page count, your data layer and your hosting all change the number.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The part that trips people up is in independent migration guidance from iqx.dev: existing applications do not automatically switch to the new caching model, and teams should review caching, navigation and configuration behavior explicitly. So after &lt;code&gt;npm install next@16.4&lt;/code&gt; your app keeps behaving the way it did. A fresh project scaffolded the same afternoon behaves differently.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cheapest way to feel that difference is to scaffold a throwaway 16.4 app next to your real one and build something tiny in it, like this &lt;a href="https://mudassirkhan.me/blog/build-calculator-nextjs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;small Next.js calculator project&lt;/a&gt;. Ten minutes in the new defaults teaches you more than an hour of reading release notes, and you get a clean reference to diff against when your real app does something surprising.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Cache Components change the migration contract
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the older App Router model you mostly reasoned about caching per route. A route was static or dynamic, and you nudged it with route segment config and &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; options.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With Cache Components you reason per component and per function. Anything you want cached, you mark with the &lt;code&gt;use cache&lt;/code&gt; directive and give it a lifetime and tags. Anything that reads request data (cookies, headers, search params) renders at request time and streams in behind a &lt;code&gt;Suspense&lt;/code&gt; boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Foqfy83a6ajc9lddm2a34.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Foqfy83a6ajc9lddm2a34.gif" alt="Comparison of route level caching (route segment config, fetch cache options, whole route static or dynamic) with Cache Components (use cache per component or function, cacheLife and cacheTag, dynamic parts stream in Suspense); a spotlight moves from the before card to the Cache Components card as each card object grows, then the band You choose what gets cached, explicitly is highlighted" width="720" height="405"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The opt in is one line of config:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// next.config.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;NextConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;next&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;nextConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;NextConfig&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;cacheComponents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;nextConfig&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The real work is what that line forces you to write next. Here is a product page that mixes shared catalog data with a per visitor cart badge:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/products/[id]/page.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cookies&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;next/headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cacheLife&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cacheTag&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;next/cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/lib/db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getProduct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;use cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cacheLife&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;hours&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;cacheTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`product:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;findUnique&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;CartBadge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cartId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cookies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;cart_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cartId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;cartItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cartId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"badge"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ProductPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;params&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getProduct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;h1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"badge"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;0&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;span&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;CartBadge&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Suspense&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two decisions live in that file that no tool can make for you. &lt;code&gt;cacheLife("hours")&lt;/code&gt; says a product description may be up to hours old. The &lt;code&gt;Suspense&lt;/code&gt; boundary says the cart badge is per visitor and must never be cached. Get the first one wrong and customers see stale prices. Get the second one wrong and one visitor sees another visitor's cart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Invalidation follows the tags. When an admin edits a product, a server action expires exactly that entry:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// app/admin/products/actions.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;use server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;revalidateTag&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;next/cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/lib/db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;updateProductPrice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;product&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;update&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;price&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;revalidateTag&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`product:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The exact signatures of &lt;code&gt;cacheLife&lt;/code&gt; profiles and &lt;code&gt;revalidateTag&lt;/code&gt; have moved between 16.x releases, so check them against the docs for the version you install before you copy this into a real codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My view after reading through the release and the migration notes: treat every &lt;code&gt;use cache&lt;/code&gt; you add as a written promise about freshness. If you cannot say out loud how stale a piece of data is allowed to be, you are not ready to cache it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What next upgrade --agent can and cannot validate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent mode is aimed at a real pain: upgrades are full of mechanical edits that are boring for people and easy for a coding agent. Run it like this, on a branch:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;git checkout &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-b&lt;/span&gt; upgrade/next-16-4
npx next upgrade &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--agent&lt;/span&gt;
npm run build
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In my opinion the line between what you hand the agent and what you keep is simple: the agent can make the build pass, but only you know whether the passing build is correct.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxnurqixf5wpglpobn5on.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fxnurqixf5wpglpobn5on.gif" alt="Two columns build in order: the agent can apply codemods, fix build errors and add Suspense boundaries; the human decides how fresh each page must be, what data is per visitor and when to ship; then the band The agent makes it build. You decide if it is right. wipes in" width="760" height="428"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What the agent is good at:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;applying codemods and renaming moved imports&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;reading build errors and fixing the obvious ones&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;wrapping request time reads in a &lt;code&gt;Suspense&lt;/code&gt; boundary when the build demands it&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What it cannot know:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;how fresh each page has to be for your business (that is the &lt;code&gt;cacheLife&lt;/code&gt; value)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;which data belongs to one visitor and must never be shared&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;whether a navigation or prefetch change breaks a flow your users depend on&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;when it is safe to ship&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the same split I argue for with any agent that touches production: give it narrow, reviewable permissions and keep the judgment calls with people. Our guide to &lt;a href="https://mudassirkhan.me/blog/mcp-enterprise-agents" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MCP architecture for enterprise agents&lt;/a&gt; goes deeper on drawing those boundaries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A practical rule: review the agent's diff with one question per changed file. "Did this edit change what gets cached, or for how long?" If yes, a human signs off on that file.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A production migration checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Upgrade the version and adopt Cache Components in two separate deploys. Then run the loop below for each route group until the whole app is on the new model.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fdwucya37rr6bum4q4kap.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fdwucya37rr6bum4q4kap.gif" alt="Upgrade validation loop with four steps that build in order: 01 Upgrade the version, 02 Build and read errors, 03 Mark cache boundaries, 04 Compare in staging, then the band Repeat for each route group" width="760" height="428"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Step&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What you do&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Stop the rollout if&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1. Upgrade the version&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Install 16.4, keep &lt;code&gt;cacheComponents&lt;/code&gt; off, deploy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Anything changes that you did not expect&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;2. Build and read errors&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Turn &lt;code&gt;cacheComponents&lt;/code&gt; on in a branch and run &lt;code&gt;next build&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;You cannot explain an error&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;3. Mark cache boundaries&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Add &lt;code&gt;use cache&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;cacheLife&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;cacheTag&lt;/code&gt; per component, &lt;code&gt;Suspense&lt;/code&gt; around request data&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A page mixes shared and per visitor data with no boundary&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;4. Compare in staging&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Diff responses and headers against production, test navigation and prefetch&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Any per visitor data shows up in a cached response&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A quick staging check for the scariest failure, a cached response that leaks visitor data:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Two different carts, same product URL. The badge must differ.&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Cookie: cart_id=cart_a"&lt;/span&gt; https://staging.example.com/products/42 | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'class="badge"&amp;gt;[0-9]*'&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-H&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Cookie: cart_id=cart_b"&lt;/span&gt; https://staging.example.com/products/42 | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'class="badge"&amp;gt;[0-9]*'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If both lines print the same count for carts that hold different items, something per visitor got cached. Stop and find the missing boundary before you go further.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then measure. Record build time and shipped JavaScript before and after, the same way the Roboto Studio write up did, so you know whether your app sees anything like their numbers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What changed in the Next.js 16.4 Cache Components upgrade?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Cache Components became the recommended path and are on by default for new &lt;code&gt;create-next-app&lt;/code&gt; projects, and the release added &lt;code&gt;next upgrade --agent&lt;/code&gt;. Existing apps keep their current caching behavior until you opt in.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How should teams adopt the Next.js 16.4 Cache Components upgrade?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
Ship the version bump first with Cache Components off. Then enable them on a branch, mark cache boundaries one route group at a time, and compare against production in staging before each rollout.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Will 16.4 make my build faster?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
One independent production write up saw a 1,555 page build drop from 83.9s to 49.1s and roughly 5% less JavaScript. Measure your own app before you count on it.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;Related reading: &lt;a href="https://mudassirworks.hashnode.dev/next-js-16-caching-demystified-what-changed-what-broke-and-how-to-fix-it" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Next.js 16 caching demystified: what changed, what broke and how to fix it&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://dev.to/mudassirworks/react-server-components-and-ai-streaming-in-nextjs-patterns-that-actually-work-35pc"&gt;React Server Components and AI streaming in Next.js&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;If you want a team to plan and run this migration on a real product, &lt;a href="https://mudassirkhan.me/services/nextjs-for-ai-products" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Next.js development for AI products&lt;/a&gt; is exactly the kind of work I take on.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Drop a comment with how you are splitting the upgrade: one big switch, or route group by route group? Curious what is working in production.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use error tracking for grouped exceptions, and use structured logs for the steps that explain a nightly catalog run. &lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; give both records the same &lt;code&gt;trace_id&lt;/code&gt;, but don't ask either system to do the other's job. That split keeps a repeated parser crash actionable without hiding a quiet business failure such as a completed import with zero active SKUs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The deciding constraint is signal quality. In this pipeline, another event isn't automatically another signal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


When should a Node.js SaaS use error tracking or structured logging?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An exception tracker answers a narrow question well: which failures recur, and which issue should an engineer triage first? Unhandled exceptions, repeated failures, release context, and environment differences belong there. Grouping matters because 8,000 failed product rows caused by one parser defect should not become 8,000 independent investigations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Structured logs answer a different question. They preserve the business path: the supplier feed arrived, 12,400 rows were read, 86 were rejected, inventory was committed, and the run ended. They also expose non-crash failures. A job can return normally while producing an empty catalog. There may be no exception to group.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I treat the telemetry budget as two queues. The issue queue gets failures that need ownership. The search index gets facts needed to reconstruct a run. Sending validation noise to the first queue destroys prioritization; sending bare stack traces only to the second throws away issue grouping across releases and environments.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Correlation doesn't turn the pair into tracing. A shared &lt;code&gt;trace_id&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;span_id&lt;/code&gt; lets an engineer move between related records, but it does not provide a distributed-trace query or a span-tree view. Be precise about that boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's the trade-off.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Start with a signal contract, not a vendor config
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The smallest useful contract has one run-level correlation value and a deliberately short list of stable business fields. This is the implementation I would put behind adapters first. It writes JSON Lines to standard output and sends the same event object to one of the two verified Infrai routes. The payload stays typed as &lt;code&gt;unknown&lt;/code&gt; at the transport boundary because each route's public discovery schema, rather than hand-written guesses, should define the application adapter.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;node:crypto&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;INFRAI_API_KEY is required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;INFRAI_BASE_URL is required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RunContext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;trace_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;run_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nl"&gt;supplier_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ProductRow&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;sku&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;boolean&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ErrorTracker&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;captureException&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RunContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ingestLog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;baseUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/v1/logs/ingest`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;POST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;Authorization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Bearer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;apiKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Content-Type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;application/json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Idempotency-Key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;idempotencyKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;429&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`Infrai &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;retry-after&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;delayMs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isFinite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;retryAfter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;_000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;250&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;2&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;**&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;attempt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;delayMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeLog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RunContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Record&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;timestamp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toISOString&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;fields&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;stdout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;write&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;\n`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ingestLog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;run_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;importCatalog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;tracker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ErrorTracker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;supplierId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nx"&gt;loadRows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ProductRow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;RunContext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;trace_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;run_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;randomUUID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;supplier_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;supplierId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeLog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;catalog_import_started&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadRows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;activeRows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;active&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;activeRows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeLog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;catalog_import_empty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;row_count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeLog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;catalog_import_completed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;row_count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;active_row_count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;activeRows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="na"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;caught&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;writeLog&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;catalog_import_failed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;error_name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;tracker&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;captureException&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Three choices are doing real work here. &lt;code&gt;trace_id&lt;/code&gt; joins the exception and log records. &lt;code&gt;run_id&lt;/code&gt; distinguishes retries or separate executions. &lt;code&gt;supplier_id&lt;/code&gt; makes one bad feed searchable without putting a product record into every event. The empty-feed branch is a log because the program behaved as written; whether zero active rows should page someone is a business rule, not proof of a crash.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep payloads lean. Customer records, access tokens, payment details, and credentials should never hitch a ride in context. OWASP recommends excluding, masking, sanitizing, hashing, or encrypting sensitive data as appropriate. That decision belongs in the contract, before the first sink receives production traffic.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Compare the investigation path, not feature counts
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would test each option with the same two fixtures: 100 occurrences of an identical parser exception and one successful run containing zero active products. Then inspect the resulting issue count, field search, release or environment context, and the number of transitions required to get from a grouped exception to its run history. No synthetic throughput claim is needed. This benchmark measures the actual debugging job.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Where it fits this pipeline&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Boundary that can change the decision&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sentry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Start here when grouped exceptions and issue triage across releases dominate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Verify that the log workflow gives enough business-step context for catalog investigations&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Datadog&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Consider it when the team wants logs and error tracking inside a broader observability product&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The wider product surface may be more configuration than a small pipeline needs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Elastic Observability&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Strong candidate when structured events already land in Elastic and schema control matters&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The team owns more ingestion, mapping, and operational choices&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Better Stack&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Worth evaluating when logs, incident response, and uptime workflows should sit close together&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Test exception grouping with the parser fixture instead of assuming log search replaces it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Infrai&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fits when error capture and log ingestion should share one REST contract with other backend capabilities&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;It has no span tree, alert delivery, source-map decoding, crash symbolication, or Session Replay&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This isn't a universal ranking. Existing operational gravity counts. A team already running Elastic needs evidence before adding another log index. A team whose investigations already live in Datadog may prefer shared context there. Sentry is the sharper shortlist entry when release-aware exception triage is the center of the job. Better Stack deserves the same fixture-based test if its adjacent incident and uptime workflow is attractive.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Infrai provides &lt;strong&gt;one API key, one bill, and one REST API with no SDK to install&lt;/strong&gt; across its backend capabilities, so the nightly worker does not accumulate a new credential and vendor account each time the team adds a service. Any language or runtime can make the HTTP call. That surface reaches 295 routes across 20 modules. Public, self-describing discovery includes request and response schemas, billing metadata, and runnable examples, and every documented capability has examples in 10 languages. For this e-commerce team, the payoff is less integration glue around error capture, log ingestion, and later backend work. Those are DX advantages, not substitutes for the missing observability features.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The limitations are material. Infrai is not suitable when native alert delivery, a distributed span tree, source-map decoding, crash symbolication, Session Replay, per-user log deletion, bulk export, or subscriptions are requirements. Choose Sentry when decoded exception triage is the main job, Datadog when a broader managed observability environment matters, or Elastic when the team needs deep control of an existing data plane. Also reject any tool whose data lifecycle cannot satisfy the store's deletion policy. A clean ingest call cannot repair that mismatch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Detection is a separate system
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Neither an exception nor a completion log can report a job that never started. The absence is silent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a heartbeat monitor such as Healthchecks for the nightly schedule. Likewise, ingestion alone does not imply threshold rules, phone calls, SMS, or webhook notifications. If the selected surface has no notification route, polling a read API and forwarding state to an alerting system becomes owned integration code. Put that code on the architecture diagram and in the maintenance estimate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction also prevents an ugly workaround: emitting a fake exception merely to make a missed business threshold visible. That corrupts the issue queue. Detection should notice missing runs and breached rules; error tracking should group actual failures; logs should retain the evidence used to explain them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What changes when the pipeline grows
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Standardize field names before adding fields. Elastic Common Schema can provide a vocabulary even when Elastic is not the destination. Add &lt;code&gt;span_id&lt;/code&gt; only when there is a real parent-child operation to represent. Decorative identifiers raise cardinality and confuse readers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Next, write retention and deletion requirements down before logs contain user-linked data. The lack of per-user deletion, bulk export, subscriptions, or configurable cold-storage controls can be disqualifying. This is a product requirement, not cleanup for later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Finally, sample expected business noise and preserve exception fidelity. Rejected rows may need counts and a bounded set of reason codes in logs. The thrown parser error still needs enough context for grouping and triage. Don't duplicate a full product payload into both places.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The durable rule is simple: exceptions become owned issues, pipeline steps become searchable events, and one correlation ID joins the investigation.&lt;/strong&gt; Add full tracing when cross-service timing and causality become the question. Until then, extra telemetry is often just extra noise.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.sentry.io/product/issues/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sentry Issues documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://docs.datadoghq.com/error_tracking/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Datadog Error Tracking documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.elastic.co/guide/en/ecs/current/index.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Elastic Common Schema reference&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://betterstack.com/docs/logs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Better Stack Logs documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://healthchecks.io/docs/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Healthchecks documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Functions, Joins and Common Table Expressions Through Hospital Data&lt;br&gt;
Introduction&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Working with a relational database involves more than retrieving records from individual tables. In practice, analysts need to combine information from different sources, compare records within groups, identify patterns over time and build queries that remain understandable as the analysis becomes more complex. SQL joins, window functions and Common Table Expressions (CTEs) provide different ways of solving these problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hospital database used in this article models patients, doctors, departments, diagnoses, procedures, wards and hospital visits. The visits table is particularly important because it connects patient activity to clinical and administrative information. Each visit records details such as the visit date, attending doctor, department, diagnosis, procedure, admission type, length of stay, insurance provider, total bill and patient satisfaction score.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This structure makes it possible to move from simple questions, such as how much a patient was billed, to more meaningful analytical questions. Which doctor generates the highest billing? How does a patient's current bill compare with their previous visit? Which departments contribute the most revenue? How does a visit compare with other visits within the same department?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Answering these questions requires an understanding of how SQL processes relationships, groups and analytical calculations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The examples below use PostgreSQL and the tables defined in the evenig schema.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Understanding SQL joins through the relationships between hospital tables&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A relational database avoids storing all information in one enormous table. Instead, information is separated into related tables, with primary and foreign keys establishing the relationships between them. This reduces duplication and makes the data easier to maintain, but it also means that meaningful analysis often requires combining multiple tables.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider the visits table. A visit contains a patient_id, but this identifier does not tell us the patient's name, county or age. Those attributes reside in the patients table. Similarly, the visit contains a doctor_id, while the doctor's name and specialty are stored in doctors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The join operation allows these records to be brought together through their relationships.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SELECT&lt;br&gt;
v.visit_id,&lt;br&gt;
v.visit_date,&lt;br&gt;
p.patient_name,&lt;br&gt;
p.county,&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_name,&lt;br&gt;
v.admission_type,&lt;br&gt;
v.total_bill_kes&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.visits AS v&lt;br&gt;
INNER JOIN evenig.patients AS p&lt;br&gt;
ON v.patient_id = p.patient_id&lt;br&gt;
INNER JOIN evenig.doctors AS d&lt;br&gt;
ON v.doctor_id = d.doctor_id&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY v.visit_date, v.visit_id;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The query connects three tables to produce a single view of hospital activity. The visits table acts as the starting point, while patients and doctors provide descriptive information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ON conditions are what make these relationships meaningful. Matching v.patient_id to p.patient_id ensures that a visit is associated with the correct patient. Matching v.doctor_id to d.doctor_id then identifies the doctor responsible for that visit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An important detail is that a patient can appear more than once in the output. Amina Otieno, for example, appears in visits 1001 and 1011. This is not a duplication error. It represents two separate visits by the same patient. The unit of analysis remains the visit, not the patient.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This distinction becomes critical when calculating totals. Counting rows in the joined result measures visits, whereas counting distinct patient identifiers measures unique patients.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How different join strategies change the result&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An INNER JOIN returns records for which the join condition matches on both sides. In this dataset, the foreign key constraints on visits.patient_id and visits.doctor_id ensure that each valid visit references an existing patient and doctor. Consequently, joining those tables through their keys should preserve the visit records.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A LEFT JOIN, however, is useful when the analysis must retain all records from a chosen starting table, even if related information is missing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, a hospital administrator might want to identify doctors who have not attended any recorded visits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SELECT&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_id,&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_name,&lt;br&gt;
COUNT(v.visit_id) AS total_visits&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.doctors AS d&lt;br&gt;
LEFT JOIN evenig.visits AS v&lt;br&gt;
ON d.doctor_id = v.doctor_id&lt;br&gt;
GROUP BY&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_id,&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_name&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY total_visits DESC;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Starting from doctors ensures that every doctor is represented, including a doctor with no matching visit. For such a doctor, columns from visits would contain NULL.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The choice of COUNT(v.visit_id) is deliberate. Since visit_id is non-null for actual visits, the expression counts matched visits but ignores the null placeholder produced by the left join. Using COUNT(*) instead would count the preserved doctor row even when no visit matched, incorrectly producing one visit for that doctor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same principle applies to other hospital tables. A department can be joined to visits through department_id, a diagnosis through diagnosis_id, and a procedure through procedure_id.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The difference between an inner join and a left join is therefore not merely syntactical. It determines which records remain visible in the result and, consequently, which entities are included in the analysis.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Joining multiple tables for a hospital performance report&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A more realistic analytical query combines several relationships to create a report that clinicians and administrators can interpret.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SELECT&lt;br&gt;
v.visit_id,&lt;br&gt;
v.visit_date,&lt;br&gt;
p.patient_name,&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_name,&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_name,&lt;br&gt;
dg.diagnosis_name,&lt;br&gt;
pr.procedure_name,&lt;br&gt;
v.admission_type,&lt;br&gt;
v.length_of_stay_days,&lt;br&gt;
v.total_bill_kes,&lt;br&gt;
v.patient_satisfaction_score&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.visits AS v&lt;br&gt;
JOIN evenig.patients AS p&lt;br&gt;
ON v.patient_id = p.patient_id&lt;br&gt;
JOIN evenig.doctors AS d&lt;br&gt;
ON v.doctor_id = d.doctor_id&lt;br&gt;
JOIN evenig.department AS dep&lt;br&gt;
ON v.department_id = dep.department_id&lt;br&gt;
LEFT JOIN evenig.diagnosis AS dg&lt;br&gt;
ON v.diagnosis_id = dg.diagnosis_id&lt;br&gt;
LEFT JOIN evenig.procedures AS pr&lt;br&gt;
ON v.procedure_id = pr.procedure_id&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY v.visit_date, v.visit_id;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result is a visit-level dataset that can support reporting, dashboard development and further analysis. The inner joins are appropriate for the required patient, doctor and department relationships. The left joins allow a visit to remain visible if diagnosis or procedure information is absent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The distinction matters because a missing diagnosis should not automatically cause a valid financial record to disappear from a hospital billing report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is another subtle issue with joins: they can change the number of rows. Joining a visit to a table with several matching records can multiply that visit in the result. This commonly occurs when analysts join a transaction table to a one-to-many relationship without considering its cardinality.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If a visit has two associated procedures in a separate procedure-details table, for example, the visit may appear twice. Summing the original visit bill after that join would overstate revenue. Analysts must therefore understand the relationship between tables and verify the grain of the result before aggregating financial measures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SQL window functions: analysing records without losing their detail&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Aggregation is useful when a report requires one result per group. A query using GROUP BY doctor_id, for instance, can calculate total revenue for each doctor. However, the individual visit records disappear from the final output because they have been reduced to grouped results.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Window functions solve a different problem. They perform calculations across related rows while retaining the individual rows in the result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This makes them particularly useful for rankings, running totals, comparisons with previous records and identifying the relative position of each visit within a group.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ranking doctors' visits by billing value&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Suppose the hospital wants to identify the highest-billed visits handled by each doctor. A simple descending sort would show the largest bills across the entire hospital, but it would not establish each doctor's position relative to their own visits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SELECT&lt;br&gt;
v.visit_id,&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_name,&lt;br&gt;
v.total_bill_kes,&lt;br&gt;
RANK() OVER (&lt;br&gt;
PARTITION BY v.doctor_id&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY v.total_bill_kes DESC&lt;br&gt;
) AS billing_rank&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.visits AS v&lt;br&gt;
JOIN evenig.doctors AS d&lt;br&gt;
ON v.doctor_id = d.doctor_id&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_name,&lt;br&gt;
billing_rank;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The window definition has two important parts. PARTITION BY v.doctor_id separates the visits into independent groups, one for each doctor. ORDER BY v.total_bill_kes DESC ranks the visits within each group from the largest bill to the smallest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rank resets for every doctor. A bill of KSh 42,000 could rank highly among one doctor's visits without necessarily being one of the highest bills across the entire hospital.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The choice of ranking function also matters. RANK() assigns equal ranks to tied values and leaves gaps in subsequent ranks. DENSE_RANK() also assigns equal ranks to ties but does not leave gaps. ROW_NUMBER() assigns a unique sequential number to every row, even when bills are equal. If a stable ordering is needed, a secondary column such as visit_id can be added to the window's ORDER BY.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These functions answer different questions. Ranking identifies relative position, while row numbering identifies a particular sequence of records.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Comparing each visit with the previous visit&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Window functions become even more valuable when the order of records matters. Suppose a hospital analyst wants to compare each patient's current bill with their previous recorded bill.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SELECT&lt;br&gt;
v.patient_id,&lt;br&gt;
p.patient_name,&lt;br&gt;
v.visit_id,&lt;br&gt;
v.visit_date,&lt;br&gt;
v.total_bill_kes,&lt;br&gt;
LAG(v.total_bill_kes) OVER (&lt;br&gt;
PARTITION BY v.patient_id&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY v.visit_date, v.visit_id&lt;br&gt;
) AS previous_bill,&lt;br&gt;
v.total_bill_kes&lt;br&gt;
- LAG(v.total_bill_kes) OVER (&lt;br&gt;
PARTITION BY v.patient_id&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY v.visit_date, v.visit_id&lt;br&gt;
) AS bill_difference&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.visits AS v&lt;br&gt;
JOIN evenig.patients AS p&lt;br&gt;
ON v.patient_id = p.patient_id&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY&lt;br&gt;
p.patient_name,&lt;br&gt;
v.visit_date,&lt;br&gt;
v.visit_id;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LAG() accesses a value from a preceding row in the same window partition. Here, the partition is the patient, so a patient's bill is compared with that patient's previous visit rather than with another patient's bill.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For Amina Otieno, visit 1001 has a bill of KSh 3,500 and visit 1011 has a bill of KSh 4,200. The second visit therefore has a bill difference of KSh 700.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first visit for each patient has no preceding visit within the available data, so previous_bill and bill_difference return NULL. This is a meaningful result, not an error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ordering also needs to be deterministic. Ordering by date alone may be insufficient if several visits occur on the same date. Including visit_id provides a tie-breaker for this dataset. In a production system, the analyst should use the actual event timestamp or another reliable sequence field where necessary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The comparison reveals a change in the recorded bill, but it does not establish why the bill changed. A higher bill could result from a different diagnosis, procedure, admission type or treatment plan. SQL produces the evidence; further analysis is required to explain the cause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Calculating cumulative hospital revenue&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A running total provides another example of a window function preserving the detail of the underlying records.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SELECT&lt;br&gt;
visit_id,&lt;br&gt;
visit_date,&lt;br&gt;
total_bill_kes,&lt;br&gt;
SUM(total_bill_kes) OVER (&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY visit_date, visit_id&lt;br&gt;
ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING&lt;br&gt;
AND CURRENT ROW&lt;br&gt;
) AS cumulative_billing&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.visits&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY visit_date, visit_id;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The cumulative total starts with the first visit and increases as each subsequent visit is processed. It allows an analyst to examine how recorded billing accumulates over the observation period.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The explicit ROWS frame is significant. It specifies that the calculation should include every row from the beginning of the ordered result through the current row. Without an explicit frame, some window aggregates use a default frame that can treat rows with equal ordering values as peers. Stating the intended frame makes the calculation easier to understand and reduces ambiguity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result is not a daily revenue summary. It is a visit-level sequence of cumulative billing. If the objective is to report one total per day, a grouped aggregation would be more appropriate.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Window functions are most effective when the question requires context across multiple records but the output must retain the individual observations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Common Table Expressions: building complex queries in understandable stages&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As analytical questions become more demanding, a single SQL statement can become difficult to read. The problem is particularly noticeable when a calculation must first be performed at one level, such as per doctor, before another calculation is applied at a different level, such as ranking doctors.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Common Table Expression, or CTE, addresses this by allowing a query to define a named intermediate result using the WITH clause. The main query can then reference that result as though it were a temporary relation available within the statement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Consider the question of which doctors generate the highest total billing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WITH doctor_revenue AS (&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_id,&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_name,&lt;br&gt;
SUM(v.total_bill_kes) AS total_revenue,&lt;br&gt;
COUNT(v.visit_id) AS total_visits&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.doctors AS d&lt;br&gt;
JOIN evenig.visits AS v&lt;br&gt;
ON d.doctor_id = v.doctor_id&lt;br&gt;
GROUP BY&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_id,&lt;br&gt;
d.doctor_name&lt;br&gt;
)&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
doctor_name,&lt;br&gt;
total_revenue,&lt;br&gt;
total_visits,&lt;br&gt;
RANK() OVER (&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY total_revenue DESC&lt;br&gt;
) AS revenue_rank&lt;br&gt;
FROM doctor_revenue&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY revenue_rank, doctor_name;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CTE first establishes a doctor-level result. Each doctor has one row containing total billing and visit count. The outer query then ranks those doctor-level totals.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The two stages operate at different grains. The inner query reduces visit-level data to one record per doctor, while the outer query compares those resulting doctor records. This separation is essential: ranking individual visits would answer a different question from ranking doctors by their total revenue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CTE makes the analytical logic visible. A reader can inspect the revenue calculation independently from the ranking calculation, and changes to one stage are easier to reason about.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A CTE is not, however, automatically a permanent table or a guaranteed performance optimisation. It exists for the duration of the SQL statement. PostgreSQL may inline a CTE into the surrounding query or materialise its result depending on the query, the PostgreSQL version and the planner's decisions. Performance should be assessed using the actual execution plan rather than assumed from the presence of WITH.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Chaining CTEs for a more complete analysis&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CTEs can also be chained so that one stage builds on another. For example, an analyst may calculate department revenue first and then compare each department with the hospital-wide average.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WITH department_revenue AS (&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_id,&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_name,&lt;br&gt;
SUM(v.total_bill_kes) AS total_revenue&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.department AS dep&lt;br&gt;
JOIN evenig.visits AS v&lt;br&gt;
ON dep.department_id = v.department_id&lt;br&gt;
GROUP BY&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_id,&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_name&lt;br&gt;
),&lt;br&gt;
hospital_average AS (&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
AVG(total_revenue) AS average_department_revenue&lt;br&gt;
FROM department_revenue&lt;br&gt;
)&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
dr.department_name,&lt;br&gt;
dr.total_revenue,&lt;br&gt;
ha.average_department_revenue,&lt;br&gt;
dr.total_revenue&lt;br&gt;
- ha.average_department_revenue AS difference_from_average&lt;br&gt;
FROM department_revenue AS dr&lt;br&gt;
CROSS JOIN hospital_average AS ha&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY dr.total_revenue DESC;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first CTE calculates total revenue per department. The second derives the average of those department totals. The final query compares each department with that average.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CROSS JOIN is appropriate because hospital_average returns a single row, which is attached to each department record. The comparison is between department totals, not individual visit bills.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That distinction illustrates why analytical stages should be designed deliberately. The average of department revenues is not the same as the average visit bill. One gives each department equal weight; the other gives each visit equal weight.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CTEs versus subqueries: two approaches to structuring the same logic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A subquery is a query nested inside another SQL statement. It can appear in a FROM clause, a WHERE condition, a SELECT expression or other permitted positions. CTEs and subqueries can often solve the same analytical problem, but their structure affects how easily a query can be understood and maintained.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take the task of identifying departments whose total billing exceeds the average department revenue.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Using a CTE, the calculation can be separated into named stages:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WITH department_revenue AS (&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
department_id,&lt;br&gt;
SUM(total_bill_kes) AS total_revenue&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.visits&lt;br&gt;
GROUP BY department_id&lt;br&gt;
)&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_name,&lt;br&gt;
dr.total_revenue&lt;br&gt;
FROM department_revenue AS dr&lt;br&gt;
JOIN evenig.department AS dep&lt;br&gt;
ON dep.department_id = dr.department_id&lt;br&gt;
WHERE dr.total_revenue &amp;gt; (&lt;br&gt;
SELECT AVG(total_revenue)&lt;br&gt;
FROM department_revenue&lt;br&gt;
)&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY dr.total_revenue DESC;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The department_revenue CTE defines the department-level totals once. Both the outer query and the scalar subquery refer to those totals, making the intended level of comparison explicit.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same logic can be expressed with nested subqueries:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SELECT&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_name,&lt;br&gt;
dr.total_revenue&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.department AS dep&lt;br&gt;
JOIN (&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
department_id,&lt;br&gt;
SUM(total_bill_kes) AS total_revenue&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.visits&lt;br&gt;
GROUP BY department_id&lt;br&gt;
) AS dr&lt;br&gt;
ON dr.department_id = dep.department_id&lt;br&gt;
WHERE dr.total_revenue &amp;gt; (&lt;br&gt;
SELECT AVG(x.total_revenue)&lt;br&gt;
FROM (&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
department_id,&lt;br&gt;
SUM(total_bill_kes) AS total_revenue&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.visits&lt;br&gt;
GROUP BY department_id&lt;br&gt;
) AS x&lt;br&gt;
)&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY dr.total_revenue DESC;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both statements express the same fundamental calculation: aggregate visits to the department level, calculate the average of those totals, and retain departments above that average. The subquery version repeats the aggregation logic, whereas the CTE version gives that logic a reusable name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This difference becomes more important as a query grows. Repeated subqueries can make it difficult to verify whether two calculations use identical filters and grouping rules. A CTE can make each analytical stage easier to inspect and modify.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nevertheless, it would be incorrect to conclude that CTEs are always faster or that subqueries are inherently inferior. PostgreSQL's query planner can optimise many equivalent expressions similarly. A CTE may be materialised in some circumstances, potentially affecting performance, while a subquery may be flattened into a larger query plan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The more useful distinction is one of query structure and semantics. CTEs are especially valuable when the query has several logical stages, an intermediate result needs to be referenced more than once, or the analysis benefits from clear named steps. Subqueries are often concise when the nested calculation is small and used only once.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Neither technique should be selected purely for style without considering the required result, the complexity of the logic and, where performance matters, the execution plan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Bringing joins, window functions and CTEs together&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The real analytical value of these concepts emerges when they are used together. Consider a hospital report that ranks departments by total billing while also calculating each department's percentage contribution to all recorded billing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;WITH department_summary AS (&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_id,&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_name,&lt;br&gt;
COUNT(v.visit_id) AS total_visits,&lt;br&gt;
SUM(v.total_bill_kes) AS total_revenue,&lt;br&gt;
AVG(v.patient_satisfaction_score)&lt;br&gt;
AS average_satisfaction&lt;br&gt;
FROM evenig.department AS dep&lt;br&gt;
JOIN evenig.visits AS v&lt;br&gt;
ON dep.department_id = v.department_id&lt;br&gt;
GROUP BY&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_id,&lt;br&gt;
dep.department_name&lt;br&gt;
)&lt;br&gt;
SELECT&lt;br&gt;
department_name,&lt;br&gt;
total_visits,&lt;br&gt;
total_revenue,&lt;br&gt;
ROUND(average_satisfaction, 2) AS average_satisfaction,&lt;br&gt;
RANK() OVER (&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY total_revenue DESC&lt;br&gt;
) AS revenue_rank,&lt;br&gt;
ROUND(&lt;br&gt;
100.0 * total_revenue&lt;br&gt;
/ NULLIF(SUM(total_revenue) OVER (), 0),&lt;br&gt;
2&lt;br&gt;
) AS revenue_percentage&lt;br&gt;
FROM department_summary&lt;br&gt;
ORDER BY revenue_rank, department_name;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CTE combines the department and visit tables and aggregates their records into a department-level summary. The window functions then rank the departments and calculate each department's percentage of the combined revenue total.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SUM(total_revenue) OVER () calculates the total of the department revenue values across the entire result without collapsing the department rows. NULLIF(..., 0) prevents division by zero if the total is zero, and multiplying by 100.0 ensures a decimal percentage calculation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result supports two distinct interpretations. The ranking identifies the relative order of departments by billing, while the percentage indicates how much each department contributes to total recorded billing. A department can rank first without necessarily accounting for a majority of the hospital's billing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The average satisfaction score also requires careful interpretation. It is the average of the scores recorded for visits in each department, not necessarily an average of all patients' lifetime satisfaction. If the same patient has multiple visits, that patient can contribute multiple observations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is why understanding the grain of the data is just as important as understanding SQL syntax. Every metric has a unit of analysis, and changing the joins, grouping level or filtering conditions can change what that metric represents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anthropic has published results from an exploratory protein-design campaign in which &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/resources/claude-scorpion-toxin-protein-design-lab-validated-binders/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Claude generated potential minibinders for scorpion neurotoxins&lt;/a&gt;. The campaign yielded &lt;strong&gt;318,484 designs&lt;/strong&gt; across &lt;strong&gt;553,052 design-toxin pairs&lt;/strong&gt;, and 17 of the 211 designs sent for laboratory testing met Anthropic’s predefined binding criteria. It is a concrete demonstration of AI-assisted research, but it is not an antivenom: Anthropic has not tested whether the binders neutralize toxins in cells or living organisms.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/claude-scorpion-toxin-binder-design" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;official Anthropic report on Claude’s scorpion-toxin binder design&lt;/a&gt; is notable because it moves beyond a purely computational example. Claude generated candidate molecules, researchers selected designs for external lab work, and two contract research organizations tested binding. For biotech teams, the study illustrates where an AI system could potentially accelerate an early discovery workflow while also showing why wet-lab validation remains indispensable.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What Anthropic’s protein-design campaign achieved
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project focused on &lt;em&gt;de novo&lt;/em&gt; minibinders, which are small designed proteins intended to bind to specific molecular targets. Claude, working through Claude Science under supervision, designed candidates against five scorpion neurotoxins associated with four scorpion species or regions. The campaign involved four follow-up prompts and ran for roughly 64 hours.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anthropic then selected 211 candidate designs for testing by Adaptyv Bio and Twist Bioscience. The experimental setup separated candidate generation from the physical work needed to assess whether the molecules bind their intended targets. Of those selected designs, &lt;strong&gt;17 met the predefined criteria for toxin binding&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That result is meaningful as an early discovery signal, not a clinical outcome. Binding is only one property a potential therapeutic or diagnostic molecule may need. The study did not evaluate toxin neutralization in cells, efficacy in animals, safety, or any therapeutic use. Anthropic explicitly says it is not claiming to have created an antivenom.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The workflow combined several distinct activities:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Claude generated large numbers of minibinder candidates and candidate-toxin pairings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Researchers chose 211 designs for experimental follow-up.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Adaptyv Bio expressed designs in a cell-free system.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Twist Bioscience expressed Fc-fusion designs in mammalian cells and assessed binding with surface plasmon resonance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The reported laboratory outcome concerned binding only, rather than in vivo performance or neutralization.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The distinction matters. AI can expand the set of hypotheses worth testing, but it cannot replace the experimental stages that establish biological function.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Aspect&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;August 2026 Anthropic campaign&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Scorpion-toxin campaign&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reported scope&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Minibinders against multiple targets from a single prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Minibinders for five scorpion neurotoxins across four scorpion species or regions&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reported experimental result&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Data and publication for 14 of 15 successful designs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;17 of 211 tested designs met predefined binding criteria&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Interaction pattern described&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Single prompt&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Four follow-up prompts with partial autonomy under supervision&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Interpretation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Earlier evidence that Claude could support multi-target minibinder design&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A larger, toxin-focused exploratory campaign with binding validation&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The results should not be treated as a direct performance comparison between the two campaigns. They involved different targets and reported outcomes. Still, the scorpion-toxin work adds laboratory-tested evidence to the earlier &lt;a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/Claude-accelerates-protein-design" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Anthropic protein-design campaign&lt;/a&gt;, where the company reported successful minibinders against multiple targets from a single prompt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What this means for biotech research workflows
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most practical lesson is not that AI has automated therapeutic development. It has not. The study instead suggests a more bounded role: helping researchers generate and prioritize a much larger set of molecular design hypotheses before committing scarce laboratory capacity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a biotech company or research-focused team, that could change the front end of a design campaign. Rather than manually defining a limited set of candidate sequences, scientists could use an AI-assisted process to explore more candidates, select a tractable subset, and route those candidates to qualified laboratory partners. The potential value is in &lt;strong&gt;faster hypothesis generation and triage&lt;/strong&gt;, not in skipping validation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anthropic’s reported 64-hour campaign duration should also be interpreted carefully. It describes the design campaign, not a complete drug-development timeline. The lab phase still required protein expression and binding assessment, and the study does not provide a cost comparison or demonstrate end-to-end development savings. Businesses evaluating similar approaches should therefore avoid translating design speed directly into claims about lower development costs or faster clinical readiness.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The experiment also clarifies an important operating constraint. Anthropic says these capabilities are &lt;strong&gt;not broadly available&lt;/strong&gt;. The work should be viewed as a research demonstration and a signal about possible future scientific workflows, rather than a generally deployable product feature that any team can adopt today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For teams that can access &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/tools" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;comparable tools&lt;/a&gt; or collaborate with specialist providers, a disciplined workflow would keep human researchers responsible for target selection, candidate review, experimental design, and interpretation. Lab results should feed back into the next design cycle. That approach is more credible than treating model output as a finished biological result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Safety and downstream validation remain central. A binder that shows a signal in an assay has not established that it will work in a cellular system, in an animal, or in a therapeutic setting. Anthropic’s plan to release the 211 designs and associated data could give researchers material to examine the campaign more closely, but it does not change the study’s stated limitations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For businesses exploring AI in &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/services/n8n-setup" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;scientific or technical workflows&lt;/a&gt;, Scalevise can help turn promising capabilities into an evaluation plan with clear human review points, &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/services/api-system-integrations" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;practical tool selection&lt;/a&gt;, and measurable pilot goals. Our &lt;a href="https://scalevise.com/services/ai-consultancy" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;AI implementation consultancy&lt;/a&gt; focuses on identifying where AI can reduce manual research work without overstating what the technology has proven. Request an AI consultancy to assess a focused, evidence-led use case for your team.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Frequently Asked Questions
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What did Claude design in Anthropic’s scorpion-toxin study?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Claude generated &lt;em&gt;de novo&lt;/em&gt; minibinder candidates intended to bind five scorpion neurotoxins associated with four scorpion species or regions. Anthropic reported 318,484 designs and 553,052 design-toxin pairs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Did Anthropic create an antivenom?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. Anthropic says it has not validated toxin neutralization in cells or living systems and does not claim to have produced an antivenom.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How were the selected designs tested?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anthropic sent 211 designs to Adaptyv Bio and Twist Bioscience. Adaptyv Bio used a cell-free expression system, while Twist Bioscience used mammalian-cell expression of Fc fusions and surface plasmon resonance to assess binding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What was the laboratory result?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Seventeen of the 211 designs selected for testing met Anthropic’s predefined criteria for binding to the toxins. This result concerns binding, not efficacy, safety, or neutralization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Can research teams broadly access these Claude protein-design capabilities?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anthropic says the capabilities described in the study are not broadly available. The campaign is an exploratory research demonstration rather than a general product availability announcement.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Conclusion
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anthropic’s scorpion-toxin campaign offers laboratory-tested evidence that Claude can assist an early protein-design workflow, from generating candidates to prioritizing designs for experimental follow-up. The 17 binding hits are an encouraging research result, but they are not evidence of an antivenom or a replacement for cellular, animal, and other downstream validation. Its clearest significance is as a measured example of how AI may expand scientific hypothesis generation while keeping experimental evidence at the center of the process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ending the foundations stage, and keep moving forward!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before I even reached this last module, many modules way back, I already knew what I wanted to do with my calculator before I even got there! But, for that, I had to reach that point, without skipping the lessons, as it is important to understand everything. That is how the vision was planned in my mind from the start!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The calculator itself was worth the challenge! Because it is not just about math, but also about bringing all the feelings, and make it bleed, and feel with purpose. That’s why the preassigned calculator from the assignment was the guide to bring that vision come to life! Here it is about orchestrating asynchronous user inputs (the mouse, and keyboard interactions), and handling edge cases gracefully. Extras just follow the script, and move on, while I just use the base to make something of my very own!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Below is a technical breakdown of this calculator that was the finishing touch to the introduction of vanilla Javascrpt.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


1/ The Frankestein numerical set up
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do I even need to explain what a calculator is for? This is just about creating a digital one. Because if you don’t have a physical one, and you don’t want a cookie-cutter calculator that you are already provided with, why don’t you make your own? Anyways, to not make the DOM CONFUSED, we have to stay everything clearly,as always.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our structure has the following variables:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;~firstNumber: Stores the initial operand or the accumulated result of a previous operation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;~currentOperator: Tracks the active mathematical operators&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;~secondNumber: Captures the incoming operand&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;~resetDisplayNext: A Boolean flag that signals wether the next digit input should clear the screen (activated immediately after an operator is selected)&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;~justCalculated: Prevents compounding evaluation errors immediately after hitting = .  It ensures absolute precision&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Javacript by default, speciallly at vanilla level, is a true test in itself. A rigurous rounding utility was implemented. A real calculator cannot fail. Why? Because that is its purpose, its meaning, if you cannot calculate as a calculator, then what are you?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The initial stage were manageable, it was in its progress that there was more to do.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Mad with the Math &amp;amp; Safety Layering
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This can turn quickly into a horror story on how Javascript gets to handle the floating-point arithmetic. To prevent native floating-point precision errors (e.g., 0.1 + 0.2=0.30000000000000000000000000004), a custom rounding utility needs to be implemented. To escape this- by calculating the number of decimal places in the operands and applying a multiplier (e.g, multiplying by 10^n before the operation, and viding by 10^n after), the application guarantees absolute accuracy….Or at least if you code it correctly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Syntax is also important, and always look for typos!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;##3/ Ascensation towards asynchronous inputs fully&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Handling user input requires strict discipline. We implemented a unified event delegation strategy, ensuring that both keyboard keydown events and mouse clicks route through the exact same validation pipeline. Half implementation of logic, and half of philosophy, like the existential crisis that I am having as I am writing this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;/Input.sanitizer: Prevents users from inputting multiple decimal points or breaking the  parsing logic with overlapping operators.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;/Error.handler: If a user attemps to divide by zero, the system doesn’t crash; it triggers the disgraceful ERR_DIV_ZERO state, locking the interface until a reset is initiated. It’s a foolproof containment protocol.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;**&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


4 UI philosophy**
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The visual layer must feel right, or else it breaks the mood, and the atmosphere, and this is part of making a web app fully responsive. If it doesn’t look right, it cannot feel right! That is why checking countless times is crucial in here! The perfect alignment- it is all about aligning  your soul with the code. If the grid, if every line is correct, it will show.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anyways, for the assignment, I kept purely minimal, and minimalist. In a way, the follow-up project also has this type of design, because I felt that it was important to not have distractions, and the let the focus be on our star: the calculator itself. So, it is dark, and the calculator is dark too. I personally do not want something that would hurt my eyes more! The way it is just works for me.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I am sorry- personal issues aside- I cannot explain well-enough to a cookie-cutter assignment. It does not bring the same excitement than when making something of my own. Don't get me wrong, this was an exciting project, but it feels like on another level to just follow my vision with creative pursuits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Introduction
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a highly technical plant, the line is the product. Every shift produces measurable output, and every deviation shows up quickly in scrap, downtime, rework, or a missed customer date. Leading production in that environment means owning outcomes you cannot fully control: equipment behaves differently on a humid day, a supplier lot shifts its tolerances, and a new operator needs three weeks before they reach standard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over the years I have come to see the work as three connected responsibilities. First, you lead the production of several processes and are accountable for whether each line succeeds. Second, you evaluate performance regularly and advise on the operating conditions that improve efficiency and quality. Third, you lead a team of engineers and technical officers to meet quality, cost, delivery, and responsiveness requirements at the same time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None of these is unique to manufacturing. Software and AI teams face the same tensions: uptime against release speed, cost against capacity, quality against delivery dates, and specialists who need clear direction without being micromanaged. This article walks through how those responsibilities work on a plant floor and what they teach engineering leaders who build software and AI systems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Own the Line, Not Just the Schedule
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A production line can fail while every individual station looks healthy. Accountability for the line means looking at the whole flow: the constraint, the handoffs, and the places where work waits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with a small set of metrics that describe the line end to end:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Overall equipment effectiveness (OEE)&lt;/strong&gt;, combining availability, performance, and quality,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;First-pass yield&lt;/strong&gt;, the share of units that are right the first time,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Scrap and rework rates&lt;/strong&gt; by station and by shift,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Changeover time&lt;/strong&gt; and unplanned downtime,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Schedule attainment&lt;/strong&gt; against the plan for the shift.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The metric matters less than the habit. A line owner should be able to say, without preparation, which constraint limited output yesterday, what it cost, and what is being done about it. If the answer depends on someone else's spreadsheet, the ownership is not real yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The software equivalent is an end-to-end service view: latency, error rate, saturation, and deployment success for the flow a customer actually experiences, not only for each service in isolation. A team can have green dashboards for every component and still fail the customer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Evaluate Performance on a Rhythm
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Good plants run on a rhythm. Each shift has a handover. Each day has a short review of the previous day's results. Each week has a deeper look at trends, and each month has a conversation about structural changes. The rhythm turns data into decisions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A practical daily review follows a simple pattern:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What happened?&lt;/strong&gt; Compare actual output and quality against the plan, with the top three losses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Why did it happen?&lt;/strong&gt; Trace each loss to a specific cause, using the data and the people closest to the process.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What changes now?&lt;/strong&gt; Pick one or two actions with an owner and a date.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Did last week's action work?&lt;/strong&gt; Close the loop on previous commitments before opening new ones.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The key discipline is to separate the symptom from the operating condition that produced it. A rise in defects might come from a temperature setpoint drifting outside its process window, a tool wearing faster than the maintenance interval assumed, or a material change that nobody communicated. Each cause calls for a different response.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Advise on Operating Conditions, With Evidence
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most valuable recommendations in a plant are often not dramatic. They adjust the operating window: a cycle time, a pressure, a feed rate, a sequence of inspections. Small, well-evidenced changes compound over a year.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before recommending a change, I try to answer four questions:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What is the measured effect?&lt;/strong&gt; Use the control chart or capability data, not the impression from the last shift.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What is the risk?&lt;/strong&gt; Every setpoint change can move a different quality characteristic.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How will we know it worked?&lt;/strong&gt; Define the metric and the observation window before the change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How do we revert?&lt;/strong&gt; A change without a rollback path is an experiment you cannot stop.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Statistical process control is the discipline that makes this possible. Control limits show whether the process is stable, and capability indices show whether it is capable of meeting the specification. A process that is stable but not capable needs engineering work on the process itself. A process that is capable but unstable needs investigation into special causes. Mixing those two up leads to wasted effort.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same logic applies to software. Changing a connection pool size, a cache TTL, or a retry policy is an operating-condition change. Measure it, define the success criteria, and keep the rollback ready. Feature flags and canary releases are the software version of a controlled process change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Lead Engineers and Officers Toward Four Outcomes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Plant teams are usually built from engineers who design processes and technical officers who run and monitor them. Both groups are essential, and both need clear direction. The leader's job is to align them on four outcomes that often pull against each other:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Quality&lt;/strong&gt;: the product meets specification every time,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cost&lt;/strong&gt;: the work is done efficiently and waste is removed,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Delivery&lt;/strong&gt;: the customer receives what was promised, when it was promised,&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Responsiveness&lt;/strong&gt;: problems are surfaced quickly and handled without delay.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trade-offs are unavoidable. Running faster may increase scrap. Holding more inventory may protect delivery but raise cost. The leader's role is to make these trade-offs visible and decide them explicitly, rather than letting each function optimize its own metric.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three practices help:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Set shared goals, not only individual targets.&lt;/strong&gt; If the maintenance team is measured only on uptime and the quality team only on defects, both may make choices that hurt the other. Shared line-level goals create conversations instead of blame.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Make expertise visible.&lt;/strong&gt; Engineers should be able to explain their reasoning to officers, and officers should be able to explain what they see on the floor to engineers. When the two groups share the same data and vocabulary, problems are solved faster.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Protect time for improvement.&lt;/strong&gt; A team that spends every hour reacting to the last failure cannot prevent the next one. Reserve regular time for root cause analysis and for small experiments that improve the process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Responsiveness Is a Design Choice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Responsiveness is often treated as a matter of effort: people must work faster when a problem appears. In reality, responsiveness is designed into the system. It depends on how quickly signals reach the right person, how clearly escalation paths are defined, and whether people feel safe raising problems early.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In a plant, that means visual management at the line, clear andon or alert procedures, and a standard response for common failures. In software, it means actionable alerts, runbooks for known incidents, and on-call rotations that do not burn people out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A useful test: when something goes wrong at 2 a.m., does the right person know within minutes, know what to do, and have the authority to act? If the answer is no, the problem is not effort. It is design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Lessons for Software and AI Teams
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Several parts of plant leadership translate directly to engineering leadership:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Measure the flow, not only the parts.&lt;/strong&gt; End-to-end metrics expose constraints that component dashboards hide.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Run a daily rhythm.&lt;/strong&gt; Short, consistent reviews of results and actions beat occasional large reviews.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Treat operating conditions as changes.&lt;/strong&gt; Document them, measure them, and be ready to roll them back.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Align teams on shared outcomes.&lt;/strong&gt; Quality, cost, delivery, and responsiveness are not one team's problem.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Design for response.&lt;/strong&gt; Alerts, runbooks, and escalation paths determine how fast a team recovers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For AI systems, the parallels are strong. Model drift is a process shift. Evaluation sets are control charts. Human review is an inspection step. Guardrails are process limits. The discipline of a well-run plant applies directly to teams deploying AI in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


A Starting Point
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you are taking on responsibility for several lines or services, I suggest this sequence:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Agree on the end-to-end metrics that describe the flow for the next quarter.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Establish a daily review with a fixed agenda and an owner for every action.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Map the constraint and the top three sources of loss, with data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pick one operating-condition change with a clear hypothesis, a measurement plan, and a rollback path.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Align the engineering and technical teams on shared goals, and make their trade-offs explicit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review what worked, document it, and repeat.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Progress in operations comes from this rhythm more than from any single initiative.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Key Takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Own the outcome of the whole line or flow, not only individual stations or services.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use a regular review rhythm to turn data into decisions and close the loop on actions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Recommend operating-condition changes only with measured evidence, a defined success criterion, and a rollback path.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Lead engineers and officers toward shared quality, cost, delivery, and responsiveness goals, and make trade-offs explicit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Design responsiveness into the system through visible signals, clear escalation, and standard responses.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Strong leadership in a technical environment is less about heroics and more about rhythm, evidence, and shared ownership. Those habits transfer well from the plant floor to software and AI teams.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What operating metric has done the most to change how your team makes decisions? I would like to hear what has worked in your environment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hi, I'm &lt;strong&gt;Vũ Xuân Toàn (Vu Xuan Toan)&lt;/strong&gt;, a software developer and indie maker from Bắc Ninh, Vietnam, and the founder of Vu Xuan Toan Tech. In my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/vut08905/hello-im-vu-xuan-toan-vu-xuan-toan-a-software-developer-indie-maker-from-vietnam-5bko"&gt;intro post&lt;/a&gt; I mentioned &lt;strong&gt;Mầm Văn&lt;/strong&gt; — the literature-learning platform I'm building for Vietnamese middle-school students (React, TypeScript, Supabase, Gemini AI). Today I want to share the part of the project I'm most proud of, because it isn't a feature for users — it's how I keep the whole system &lt;em&gt;understandable&lt;/em&gt; while it changes every week.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The problem: the system grows faster than memory
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Mầm Văn has two big portals (teacher and student), an AI content studio, a question bank, XP and mastery engines, a knowledge tree, grading workflows, notifications, an admin area… After a few months of intense building, "how does this all connect?" stopped being a question I could answer from memory.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So I built a &lt;strong&gt;complete map of every flow in the system&lt;/strong&gt; — 262 nodes covering every screen, every engine, and the relationships between them (for example: &lt;em&gt;publishing a lesson → notifies students&lt;/em&gt;, or &lt;em&gt;rejecting a gift request → refunds the student's total XP wallet&lt;/em&gt;). Every node also lists the exact code files and functions that implement it, verified automatically against the codebase.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The rule: the map is part of the code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The map only stays true if it can never drift. So I work under one strict rule:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Before&lt;/strong&gt; changing any flow, I open the map and identify which nodes and relationships the change touches.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;After&lt;/strong&gt; the change, I update the map, regenerate it, and ship it &lt;strong&gt;in the same commit&lt;/strong&gt; as the code.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The map and a full &lt;strong&gt;change history&lt;/strong&gt; are republished automatically to my website in the same round.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can see both, live:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;🗺️ &lt;strong&gt;The system map&lt;/strong&gt; (interactive, click a node to light up its cluster): &lt;a href="https://vuxuantoan.me/so-do-mam-van.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://vuxuantoan.me/so-do-mam-van.html&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;📜 &lt;strong&gt;The change history&lt;/strong&gt; — every work session: what changed, in which files, with lines added/removed: &lt;a href="https://vuxuantoan.me/lich-su-mam-van.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://vuxuantoan.me/lich-su-mam-van.html&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Before &amp;amp; after, for every flow change
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The part I like most: whenever a change modifies a &lt;em&gt;flow&lt;/em&gt;, the history page automatically gets two buttons — &lt;strong&gt;"flow BEFORE this change"&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;"flow AFTER this change"&lt;/strong&gt;. Every version of the map is archived immutably, so I can reopen exactly how the system looked before any change and compare. When I recently removed an AI status bar from the question bank, the before-map still shows its node in the tree; the after-map doesn't. That makes design review honest: nothing about the system's shape is ever lost or misremembered.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why bother?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because an indie maker's scarcest resource isn't code — it's &lt;em&gt;clarity&lt;/em&gt;. The map has already caught wrong assumptions before I wrote a line of code, and it lets anyone (a collaborator, a teacher using the platform, future me) understand a fairly complex product in minutes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you build long-lived side projects, I'd genuinely recommend stealing this workflow: one living map, updated in the same commit as the code, with archived before/after versions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;—&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm Vũ Xuân Toàn. My home on the web is &lt;a href="https://vuxuantoan.me" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;vuxuantoan.me&lt;/a&gt;, my code lives at &lt;a href="https://github.com/vut08905" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/vut08905&lt;/a&gt;, and Mầm Văn itself is live at &lt;a href="https://mamvan.vercel.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;mamvan.vercel.app&lt;/a&gt;. Say hi!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've tried a lot of dictation tools, and almost all of them ship your audio off to someone's cloud. That always bugged me. So when I found Inaudio, a tiny desktop app that does all of it locally, I had to share it.&lt;br&gt;
You press a keyboard shortcut and talk. It types. And it keeps a searchable history of everything you've dictated, so you can go back and grab that one sentence from yesterday.&lt;br&gt;
It works the other way too. Paste text or copy something to your clipboard, and it reads it aloud with on-device speech synthesis. Great for proofreading your own writing (you hear the clunky sentences way faster than you see them).&lt;br&gt;
Under the hood it's Electron, React and TypeScript, with sherpa-onnx doing the speech work. You download a model once, then everything runs on your CPU. One of the options is NVIDIA's Nemotron streaming ASR model for English, tuned for about 560 ms latency.&lt;br&gt;
Is it perfect? No. This is a brand new project with only a handful of stars, so expect rough edges. And text insertion and permissions behave differently on Windows, macOS and Linux.&lt;br&gt;
But if you want private dictation without a subscription, it's worth a try. And honestly, it's a great time to jump in and contribute while the project is still small.&lt;/p&gt;


